{"type": "message", "message": "**Minimalist means fewer distractions—not fewer comforts.** In a small flat, it’s about choosing a coordinated set of useful furniture and leaving clear space to move.\n\n- **Simple shapes:** clean-lined furniture without bulky detailing.\n- **A restrained palette:** a few repeated colours and materials, with texture to keep it warm.\n- **Practical storage:** closed cupboards for everyday clutter, with a few favourite objects on display.\n- **Complete, purposeful zones:** comfortable seating, a reachable table and light—not just an empty room.\n- **Breathing room:** avoid filling every corner or blocking routes between doors and windows.\n\nFor your combined living–dining room, that could mean distinct sitting and eating areas linked by the same palette. Your existing warm off-white walls could be a good starting point—minimalism doesn’t require stark white or replacing everything.", "conversationId": "13373d314ad846b1a717dbbdc369d349"}
